--TEST--
`Core\Test\Failure` sits directly under `Throwable`, so a `catch (RuntimeError)` in the code being tested never matches one while a `catch (Throwable)` does, and the message names the member and both sides it compared — `rule:testing/failure-ledger`, `rule:errors/throwable-hierarchy`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:errors/assertion-failures
// `rule:testing/failure-ledger` makes catching a failure necessary rather than
// merely allowed, which puts the class's *position* in the tree on the hot
// path of every composite assertion: one level lower and the arm a test writes
// around the code it is testing would eat the failure it is there to report.

// The `RuntimeError` arm is written first on purpose. It is the arm real code
// carries, and a failure that matched it would be reported as the code under
// test failing rather than as the assertion failing.
try {
    Core\Test::assertEquals(2 + 2, 5);
    echo "unreachable: the assertion returned\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $wrong) {
    echo "unreachable: the RuntimeError arm matched\n";
} catch (Throwable $failure) {
    echo $failure->message, "\n";
}

// And the class's own name matches it, so a test that means to intercept one
// says so rather than widening to `Throwable`.
try {
    Core\Test::assertCount(["order", "customer"], 3);
} catch (Core\Test\Failure $counted) {
    echo $counted->message, "\n";
}

// `rule:core-api/no-mode-strings`'s shape parameter carries the caller's own
// sentence, and it goes in front of the member's text rather than replacing it:
// a message that replaced it would lose which member failed.
try {
    Core\Test::assertTrue(false, {message: "the flag should be set"});
} catch (Core\Test\Failure $flagged) {
    echo $flagged->message, "\n";
}

// A failure is thrown, so it carries what every throw carries: the `throw`
// site's own position, and no cause.
try {
    Core\Test::assertNull("not null");
} catch (Core\Test\Failure $nulled) {
    echo Core\Str::isEmpty($nulled->location) ? "no location" : "located", "\n";
    echo $nulled->previous == null ? "no cause" : "has a cause", "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
Core\Test::assertEquals failed: `$actual` is 4, `$expected` is 5
Core\Test::assertCount failed: `$actual` holds 2 entries, `$expected` is 3
the flag should be set: Core\Test::assertTrue failed: `$actual` is false
located
no cause
